\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Metric and mass from a coupling}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{The limiting precision symbol in \ref{fdip-01D4} is
#{\mu+4\pi^2\kappa\cdot K\kappa}. With the grid coordinates
and field amplitude fixed, \ref{fdip-01D8} recovers its mass
parameter #{\mu} and every component of #{K} from finitely
many mode variances. Other finite-range coefficients satisfying
the stated convergence and positivity hypotheses do not change
these mode or box limits. This is the sense in which these
families have one large-scale description.}

\p{On the unit torus in these coordinates, the second-order
operator is #{-\covdot K\cov}. The positive tensor
#{K} is contravariant: the corresponding constant Riemannian
metric has covariant tensor #{K^{-1}}. Its Riemannian volume
is #{(\det K)^{-1/2}} times coordinate volume. The covariance
normalization used above is coordinate volume; passing to the
Riemannian volume convention includes the corresponding constant
field normalization. In particular, metric and mass are read
from the coupling with these background conventions specified,
as discussed in \ref{fdip-00AU} and \ref{fdip-004K}.}

\p{The dimension, periodic grid, coordinate lengths, translation
invariance and field normalization were supplied in the hypotheses.
This construction does not recover an unspecified dimension or
curvature. A spatially varying metric would require couplings
that vary with position. The hierarchical scaling dimension of
the power-counting relation in \ref{fdip-01CZ} is a different datum,
read from #{p} and #{\lambda}; no identification of a hierarchy
with a periodic Euclidean grid is used.}
